{"id":"2314a79115d319ebdc6a2592f5ac342c3c68d6c4d40ea0d65165a3068a9f3d8b","pubkey":"c70b89f075cc6e011164cc68d3257a39253aca7409196e1a7690b66bd963ea74","created_at":1781784000,"content":"The Six-Layer Simulation: How Mediated Reality Hijacks Your Mind And How To Walk Back Into Reality\n\nhttps://blossom.primal.net/3a983f7d367d17c3068def05693b8b82518c4c10c0030ee2208fa0858d71772a.png\n\r\nBy Albert, A System Thinker and Inner Expansion Architect\n\n\r\n•\tThe Six-Layer Simulation: Escaping Mediated Reality And Reclaiming Your Mind\r\n•\tFeeling like reality is scripted and fake is not paranoia. It is a systems outcome. Discover the six-layer simulation shaping your mind and learn a step-by-step model to walk back into reality.\n\n\r\nSummary\r\nMost people are not living in reality anymore. They are living inside coverage of reality  headlines instead of understanding, labels instead of human beings, and performance instead of presence. This is an engineered, six-layer simulation built from symbols, algorithms, fear, narratives, and political theatre. When you see this machinery clearly, you stop reacting on cue and can redesign how you experience the world in your nervous system, your identity, and your civic life. \n\n\r\nReality has not disappeared. It is just wearing six layers of costume. This is a field guide for those ready to build a truer relationship with the world.\n\n\r\nOpening\r\nWhat if the world does not feel fake because you are cynical, but because you are accidentally living inside a six-layer simulation that was never designed for your flourishing?\n\n\r\nContext & Problem\r\nYou wake up inside a feed. Before your feet touch the ground, you have scrolled through crises, villains, and moral instructions  none of which came from your village or your body, but from systems that profit when you stay emotionally activated. Modern platforms capture attention to sell it, and since fear, outrage, and moral shock travel faster than nuance, the system tilts toward content that keeps your heart racing. Simultaneously, politics has adopted the tools of content creation; hearings are staged for viral clips, and scandals cycle as addictive dramas that rarely resolve. You are not imagining that something is off  you are inhabiting a mediated reality by default. \n\n\r\nFirst Principles Breakdown\r\nTo understand this, we must strip the simulation to physics, biology, and incentives. Humans do not relate to \"raw\" reality; we rely on internal representations and emotional tags to navigate quickly, a biological trait that makes us vulnerable to symbolic manipulation. Our brains are wired to defend tribal symbols  flags, logos, labels  often more fiercely than we defend actual human lives. Modern systems exploit this by controlling the symbols rather than the facts, knowing it is cheaper to steer perception through narrative frames than to enforce behavior through physical force. \n\n\r\nSystems Thinking Analysis: The Six Layers\r\nThese layers are not isolated; they form interlocking feedback loops:\r\n1.\tMediated Reality: We experience events primarily through media capsules rather than direct experience. \r\n2.\tHuman Conditioning: Algorithms reward engagement, forcing individuals to flatten their personalities into repeatable, performative personas. \r\n3.\tCollapse of Information Into Content: Information answers questions, but content stimulates responses to occupy attention and prevent silence. \r\n4.\tFear and Identity Control: Fear scales engagement, so algorithms amplify fear-based identity policing, creating a state of constant moral emergency. \r\n5.\tNarrative Warfare: Facts are secondary to frames that assign heroes and villains, effectively radicalizing populations through pre-packaged stories. \r\n6.\tThe Theatre of Power: Politics operates as a stage for symbolic gestures, while structural decisions remain untouched, keeping the audience emotionally occupied rather than materially informed.\n\n\r\nDesign Thinking Application: The Human Pain\r\nThe pain here is visceral: waking up exhausted by external alerts, fluent in global outrage but clumsy in local relationships, and feeling simultaneously hyper-informed and powerless. As an Inner Expansion Architect, I ask: what kind of human is this system sculpting? Design, in this context, must move beyond interfaces to address how the environment impacts our sense of wholeness. \n\n\r\nThe 5 Profound Insights\r\n1.\tYour Nervous System Is The Target: The system wants your predictable reactions, not your beliefs. Calming your nervous system is an act of civic resistance. \r\n2.\tSilence Is A Technology: The simulation fears silence because that is where thinking and value formation occur. Reclaiming unfilled time is a radical design choice. \r\n3.\tIdentity Is An Interface: When you confuse your digital profile with your essence, you become programmable. Hold your labels lightly. \r\n4.\tManipulation Leaves Facts Intact: The most effective control floods the environment with frames, not lies. Practice frame-checking every story you encounter. \r\n5.\tParticipation Changes Systems: Purity is not the answer. You escape by refusing to react on cue and by actively participating in healthier, grounded systems.\n\n\r\nNew Solution Model: The Reality Immunity Architecture\r\nReality Immunity is built on three pillars:\r\n1.\tInner Anchoring: Stabilizing your attention through body awareness and contemplative practices.\r\n2.\tNarrative Literacy: Using systems thinking to trace headlines back to underlying incentives.\r\n3.\tSystemic Participation: Engaging in governance and cooperative projects that favor structure over theatre.\n\n\r\nStep-by-Step Guide: Walking Back Into Reality\r\n1.\tAwareness: Identify the six layers in your daily feed. \r\n2.\tDiagnosis: Trace one recurring reactive pattern to its algorithmic source. \r\n3.\tReframing: Ask what need (connection, agency) you are actually trying to meet. \r\n4.\tIntervention: Design an environment change, like a weekly no-input walk. \r\n5.\tFeedback: Treat your nervous system as a living lab for two weeks. \r\n6.\tIteration: Add support or friction where needed.\r\n7.\tScaling: Translate your private sanity into shared, local culture.\n\n\r\nReal-World Example\r\nCitizen A lives in the symbol layer, sharing outrage and feeling hopeless. Citizen B treats anger as a signal, not a script, and uses it to move toward structural work  like RTI activism or policy feedback  transforming from an audience member into an author of change. \n\n\r\nFuture Implications\r\nThe cost of inaction is deeper polarization and institutional capture. The possibility is a culture of citizens who see frames, interrogate incentives, and design healthier systems. \n\n\r\nConclusion\r\nThe world is not fake; it is covered. Seeing the machinery lets you participate deeply without being captured. That is inner expansion. That is governance as architecture.\n\r\n\n\n1.\tShare this with someone who says \"something feels off.\"\r\n2.\tDesign one tiny intervention for the next 14 days.\r\n3.\tStay close  we are here to design systems for life to thrive.\n\n\r\nFAQ\r\n1.\tIs this \"fake news\"? No, it is about how truth is framed for engagement. \r\n2.\tShould I log off? Use the amplifier consciously rather than retreating. \r\n3.\tIs this a conspiracy? No, it is a system of emergent incentives.\r\n4.\tHow does this affect policy? It allows us to track structures rather than slogans. \r\n5.\tWhere do I start? With the smallest scale  one silent walk or one primary source.","tags":[["imeta","m image/png","ox 3a983f7d367d17c3068def05693b8b82518c4c10c0030ee2208fa0858d71772a","size 2218181","url https://blossom.primal.net/3a983f7d367d17c3068def05693b8b82518c4c10c0030ee2208fa0858d71772a.png","x 3a983f7d367d17c3068def05693b8b82518c4c10c0030ee2208fa0858d71772a"]]}
{"id":"a886b543b3cbb6da97d58ca727bedc6ccd18fe34a36e16d18172ab071c5d48a9","pubkey":"274a2e86f1a7acb45b01f290284a343c248cb913677b43d7f7615b513194d8b0","created_at":1781783976,"content":"https://share.yabu.me/274a2e86f1a7acb45b01f290284a343c248cb913677b43d7f7615b513194d8b0/9f6f02fc097ca83a8dcf934ea0d3011aa0cecbea09fc3554c124992039fe2169.webp","tags":[["p","274a2e86f1a7acb45b01f290284a343c248cb913677b43d7f7615b513194d8b0"],["e","be0be7ffc0544ece8e83d9db41c88938e9a4fb77694275d771b824272b8e2514","wss://yabu.me","root"],["imeta","url https://share.yabu.me/274a2e86f1a7acb45b01f290284a343c248cb913677b43d7f7615b513194d8b0/9f6f02fc097ca83a8dcf934ea0d3011aa0cecbea09fc3554c124992039fe2169.webp","m image/webp","x bf17eea1f5d090cfa47426d4ec813f5c49cc004273c98bbc3a88bc0c1238b380","ox 9f6f02fc097ca83a8dcf934ea0d3011aa0cecbea09fc3554c124992039fe2169","size 94654","dim 1280x718","blurhash U69soD}^55EOMwb:9[nN0c9t-q$+EmwgwNtP","no_transform false","visibility 1"],["r","https://share.yabu.me/274a2e86f1a7acb45b01f290284a343c248cb913677b43d7f7615b513194d8b0/9f6f02fc097ca83a8dcf934ea0d3011aa0cecbea09fc3554c124992039fe2169.webp"],["client","lumilumi","31990:84b0c46ab699ac35eb2ca286470b85e081db2087cdef63932236c397417782f5:1727506446612","wss://cagliostr.compile-error.net"]]}
{"id":"cb464f59c43f1b12d667dcdc16d9f25abdd277e3b3d42df07a493f0d7e9bef06","pubkey":"5bee7f841727ad2ea7558956252510c2f40ed7e92daf8832db7a3c72b990c6d3","created_at":1781783620,"content":"https://media.einundzwanzig.space/s/bitcoinsepp","tags":[]}
{"id":"6985bb1efc76965b682ab0d92e2e17a0d3e7284b0b733bc06b4d0549cf765f20","pubkey":"c70b89f075cc6e011164cc68d3257a39253aca7409196e1a7690b66bd963ea74","created_at":1781783563,"content":"What If Reality Is Being Replaced? The Six Layers of the Modern Simulation\n\r\nReality is no longer experienced directly. It is mediated through symbols, algorithms, narratives, and incentives. Explore the six layers of the modern simulation and how to reclaim independent thought.\n\r\nSummary\r\nMost people believe they are responding to reality.\r\nWhat if they are responding to a simulation built from symbols, incentives, algorithms, narratives, and emotional triggers?\n\r\nThis article explores six layers through which modern systems increasingly mediate human perception and behavior. Using First Principles Thinking, Systems Thinking, and Design Thinking, we examine how reality is replaced, not through conspiracy, but through incentives. More importantly, we explore how individuals can recover agency, authenticity, and direct contact with reality.\n\r\nReality rarely disappears all at once.\n\r\nIt gets covered layer by layer until symbols become more influential than substance, narratives become more powerful than facts, and performance replaces understanding.\n\r\nThe question is not whether the simulation exists.\n\r\nThe question is whether you can still see beyond it.\n\r\nWhat If Reality Is Being Replaced?\n\r\nThe Opening\r\nThe greatest deception of our age is not that people are being lied to.\n\r\nIt is that people increasingly mistake interpretation for reality itself.\n\r\nMost people no longer experience events directly. They experience headlines, clips, narratives, labels, reactions, and algorithmically selected fragments of reality.\n\r\nThe map is becoming more important than the territory.\n\r\nThe symbol is becoming more important than the thing it represents.\r\nAnd that changes everything.\r\n________________________________________\r\nThe Hidden Crisis Nobody Is Measuring\r\nSomething feels off.\n\r\nPeople are more connected than ever, yet increasingly fragmented.\n\r\nInformation has never been more abundant, yet understanding appears scarce.\r\nPolitical participation is everywhere, yet trust in institutions continues to decline.\r\nMany people cannot explain why this is happening because they are looking at isolated symptoms rather than the underlying system.\n\r\nSocial media has become a major source of news and information for millions of people worldwide. At the same time, research shows that emotionally charged content, particularly anger and outrage, spreads farther and faster than calmer forms of communication. Studies have found that social reinforcement mechanisms such as likes, shares, and engagement signals amplify moral outrage and divisive content.\n\r\nThe result is not merely an information problem.\r\nIt is a reality problem.\r\n________________________________________\r\nFirst Principles Breakdown: What Is Actually Happening?\r\nMost explanations begin in the wrong place.\n\r\nPeople assume the core problem is misinformation.\n\r\nOthers assume the problem is political polarization.\r\nSome blame technology.\r\nOthers blame media.\n\r\nFrom a First Principles perspective, all of these are secondary effects.\r\nThe fundamental question is:\r\nHow do humans determine what is real?\r\nHistorically, reality emerged primarily from direct experience.\n\r\nYou saw events.\r\nYou participated in communities.\r\nYou interacted with physical environments.\r\nYou formed beliefs slowly through observation.\r\nToday, reality increasingly arrives pre-processed.\r\nBefore you encounter an event, someone has already framed it.\r\nBefore you form an opinion, someone has already supplied a narrative.\r\nBefore you ask a question, someone has already defined the available answers.\r\nThe problem is not information.\r\nThe problem is mediation.\r\nReality is becoming increasingly filtered through layers of interpretation.\r\n________________________________________\r\nThe Six Layers of the Modern Simulation\r\nThese layers are not conspiracies.\n\r\nThey are emergent outcomes of incentives operating at scale.\r\nEach layer builds upon the previous one.\r\nTogether they create what feels like a simulation.\n\r\nLayer 1: Mediated Reality\r\nReality is no longer experienced directly.\r\nIt is experienced through screens, feeds, headlines, clips, influencers, and commentators.\r\nCoverage replaces experience.\r\nInterpretation replaces observation.\r\nThe result is a population reacting to representations rather than reality itself.\r\n\nLayer 2: Human Conditioning\r\nHumans adapt to incentives.\r\nWhatever receives attention gets repeated.\r\nWhatever receives rewards gets amplified.\r\nResearch shows that social reinforcement mechanisms encourage users to express more moral outrage because outrage receives more engagement.\r\nPeople gradually optimize themselves for visibility rather than authenticity.\r\n\nLayer 3: The Collapse of Information\r\nInformation creates understanding.\r\nContent creates engagement.\r\nThese are not the same thing.\r\nInformation resolves uncertainty.\r\nContent often sustains it.\r\nWhen engagement becomes the objective, resolution becomes undesirable because resolution ends the cycle.\r\n\nLayer 4: Fear and Identity Control\r\nHumans evolved in tribes.\r\nExclusion once threatened survival.\r\nThat wiring remains active.\r\nDigital systems can now trigger belonging, exclusion, approval, and rejection at unprecedented scale.\r\nFear becomes a highly effective behavioral steering mechanism.\r\n\nLayer 5: Narrative Warfare\r\nFacts rarely move populations.\r\nStories do.\r\nNarratives assign heroes, villains, victims, and enemies.\r\nOnce these roles are established, complexity disappears.\r\nPeople begin defending stories rather than examining reality.\r\n\nLayer 6: The Theater of Power\r\nPolitics increasingly functions as symbolic performance.\r\nVisibility becomes more important than outcomes.\r\nAttention becomes more important than problem solving.\r\nThe audience remains emotionally engaged while many root causes remain untouched.\r\n________________________________________\r\nSystems Thinking Analysis\r\nThe simulation persists because powerful feedback loops reinforce it.\n\r\nConsider the cycle:\r\nAttention → Engagement → Revenue → Amplification → More Attention\r\nEmotionally activating content generates engagement.\r\nEngagement generates revenue.\r\nRevenue incentivizes amplification.\r\nAmplification increases visibility.\r\nVisibility attracts more emotional content.\r\nThe loop strengthens itself.\n\r\nResearch consistently finds that divisive, emotionally charged, and out-group hostile content receives disproportionate amplification in engagement-based systems.\r\nThe critical insight is this:\r\nNo central coordinator is required.\r\nThe system self-organizes around incentives.\r\nThis is why simplistic explanations fail.\r\nThe problem is structural.\r\n________________________________________\r\nDesign Thinking Analysis\r\nBehind every system failure is a human need that has been misunderstood.\r\nPeople do not seek misinformation.\r\nThey seek certainty.\r\nThey do not seek tribalism.\r\nThey seek belonging.\r\nThey do not seek outrage.\r\nThey seek significance.\r\nThey do not seek constant stimulation.\r\nThey seek meaning.\n\r\nThe tragedy is that modern systems often provide substitutes rather than solutions.\r\nVisibility substitutes for recognition.\r\nPerformance substitutes for identity.\r\nEngagement substitutes for connection.\r\nContent substitutes for understanding.\r\nThe human need remains unmet.\r\nThe behavioral loop continues.\r\n________________________________________\r\nFive Profound Insights Most People Miss\r\nInsight 1: The Simulation Is Built on Incentives, Not Conspiracies\r\nMany people search for secret controllers.\r\nThe deeper reality is often simpler.\n\r\nSystems naturally evolve toward whatever is rewarded.\r\nIncentives shape outcomes more reliably than intentions.\r\nImplication\r\nChanging incentives matters more than exposing villains.\r\n________________________________________\r\nInsight 2: Symbols Are Becoming More Powerful Than Reality\r\nBrands, labels, hashtags, political identities, and social signals increasingly determine perception.\n\r\nPeople react to symbols before examining substance.\r\nImplication\r\nThe battle for perception increasingly precedes the battle for truth.\r\n________________________________________\r\nInsight 3: Engagement Is Not Understanding\r\nBeing informed and feeling informed are different experiences.\r\nMany people consume vast amounts of content while gaining little understanding.\r\nImplication\r\nInformation diets require the same intentional design as food diets.\r\n________________________________________\r\nInsight 4: Fear Is the Most Scalable Governance Technology\r\nFear accelerates compliance.\r\nFear simplifies decision-making.\r\nFear suppresses nuance.\n\r\nResearch shows anger and fear significantly increase sharing behavior and online engagement.\r\nImplication\r\nA fearful population becomes easier to steer.\r\n________________________________________\r\nInsight 5: Reality Never Disappears\r\nThis is the most hopeful insight.\r\nReality remains intact beneath the symbolic layers.\r\nThe challenge is not creating reality.\r\nThe challenge is reconnecting with it.\r\nImplication\r\nRecovery is possible.\r\n________________________________________\r\nThe Reality Recovery Model™\n\r\nTo address a systems problem, we need a systems solution.\r\nThe Reality Recovery Model consists of five interconnected capacities:\r\nAwareness\r\nPerception\r\nReflection\r\nDirect Experience\r\nAgency\r\nThe objective is simple:\r\nReduce mediation.\r\nIncrease reality contact.\r\nStrengthen independent judgment.\r\nRestore authentic participation.\r\n________________________________________\r\nA Seven-Stage Guide to Escaping the Simulation\r\n\nStage 1: Awareness\r\nRecognize that every information system contains incentives.\r\n\nStage 2: Diagnosis\r\nMap your primary sources of influence.\r\n\nStage 3: Reframing\r\nAsk what assumptions are embedded within every narrative.\r\n\nStage 4: Intervention\r\nReduce exposure to high-emotion, low-information environments.\r\n\nStage 5: Feedback\r\nObserve how your thinking changes.\r\n\nStage 6: Iteration\r\nContinuously refine your information ecosystem.\r\n\nStage 7: Scaling\r\nHelp communities build healthier reality-contact systems.\r\n________________________________________\r\nA Real-World Example\n\r\nConsider moral outrage on social media.\r\nResearchers found that expressions of outrage receive greater social reinforcement through likes and shares, causing users to learn and repeat outrage-based communication patterns.\n\r\nInitially, users believe they are expressing authentic emotion.\r\nOver time, the platform’s reward structure influences behavior.\r\nThe system rewards outrage.\r\nUsers produce more outrage.\r\nThe algorithm amplifies it.\n\r\nThe culture adapts.\r\nEventually, outrage appears normal.\r\nNo conspiracy was necessary.\r\nOnly incentives.\r\n________________________________________\r\nThe Future: Two Possible Paths\r\nOne future is obvious.\n\r\nMore mediation.\r\nMore polarization.\r\nMore identity fragmentation.\r\nMore algorithmic dependency.\r\nMore symbolic conflict.\r\nLess reality.\n\r\nThe alternative future is also possible.\r\nA society that values direct experience.\r\nA culture that rewards understanding rather than reaction.\r\nInstitutions designed around human flourishing rather than engagement metrics.\r\nCitizens capable of independent thought.\r\nCommunities capable of genuine dialogue.\n\r\nThe difference between these futures is not technology.\r\nIt is design.\r\n________________________________________\r\nConclusion\r\nThe deepest danger of the modern simulation is not that reality disappears.\r\nIt is that people forget how to find it.\r\nReality has not been destroyed.\r\nIt has been layered over.\r\nCovered by narratives.\r\nCovered by incentives.\r\nCovered by symbols.\r\nCovered by performance.\n\r\nThe task of our time is not merely to consume better information.\r\nIt is to recover direct contact with reality itself.\r\nBecause once reality becomes secondary, manipulation becomes easy.\r\nBut once reality becomes primary again, manipulation loses its power.\r\nThe goal is not to escape the world.\r\nThe goal is to see it clearly.\r\nTo think independently.\r\nTo participate consciously.\r\nTo remember that life exists beneath the symbols.\n\r\nAnd always has.\n\r\nBy Albert, A System Thinker and Inner Expansion Architect\n\r\nFrequently Asked Questions\r\n1. What is mediated reality?\r\nMediated reality refers to experiencing events primarily through media, platforms, narratives, and interpretations rather than direct observation.\r\n\n2. Is the modern simulation a conspiracy theory?\r\nNo. The framework describes how incentives, algorithms, institutions, and human psychology interact to shape perception without requiring centralized coordination.\r\n\n3. Why does outrage spread so quickly online?\r\nResearch suggests outrage receives stronger engagement signals such as likes, shares, and comments, encouraging platforms and users to amplify it.\r\n\n4. What is narrative warfare?\r\nNarrative warfare is the competition to define the meaning of events before people independently interpret them.\r\n\n5. How can people reconnect with reality?\r\nThrough direct experience, reflective thinking, community dialogue, reduced dependence on algorithmic feeds, and conscious attention management.\r\n\nSuggested Sources\r\n1.\tPew Research Center, Social Media and News Research\r\n2.\tYale University, Moral Outrage and Social Reinforcement Research\r\n3.\tNature Human Behaviour, Social Media and Political Hostility\r\n4.\tKnight First Amendment Institute, Algorithmic Amplification Research\r\n5.\tScience Advances, Social Learning and Moral Outrage\r\nhttps://blossom.primal.net/5c4f04ec84ef11ba6b783d54f2526dae86867d4dbcb4ed1d3dd2a21ef2d644e6.png","tags":[]}
{"id":"a134cf434cb1da64d4bbc370db31a22ec886c12c8ed8a962a042fc923bd9589d","pubkey":"b7cf9f42a796b091e843dce919d3ef4c0dc82e029452edf0bdbcdeb9ecb93e78","created_at":1781783159,"content":"https://www.brside.com/blog/brave-firefox-safari-only-two-survived-this-fingerprinting-test\n\nIntresting to see an article that looks into more sufisticated #browserfingerprinting\nAnd delivers best degrees for #Firefox and its derivatives.\n\n#privacy #surveillance #anonymity #freedom","tags":[["t","browserfingerprinting"],["t","firefox"],["t","privacy"],["t","surveillance"],["t","anonymity"],["t","freedom"],["client","Coracle","31990:97c70a44366a6535c145b333f973ea86dfdc2d7a99da618c40c64705ad98e322:1685968093690"]]}
{"id":"bb822bf3d388c2b9b18f306b320223d075b25740d4b1301fc8e86619eb725537","pubkey":"1a3257e040316070eb7ce9d843c394e36379b8b55a16d1e1d961c15519295462","created_at":1781783147,"content":"Looking like poppy seed ice cream 🍦  https://image.nostr.build/982f598ab3a047849901ac08d830555479eb1eb990bea34e4377d41d514211a9.jpg","tags":[["imeta","m image/jpeg","url https://image.nostr.build/4ba1644d63d31b02540a4634bd3c2a25c7e949c475c8d04e3fa0f4330a76c553.jpg"],["imeta","m image/jpeg","url https://image.nostr.build/283f49895dc8d392f7507130da80cef93f87461d27d19ff2fc815f481bee3ad0.jpg"],["imeta","m image/jpeg","url https://image.nostr.build/0b6f5ae6c8f8338a74626f37eba46922af1375f46cd13b97b89b56e4225a9546.jpg"],["imeta","m image/jpeg","url https://image.nostr.build/982f598ab3a047849901ac08d830555479eb1eb990bea34e4377d41d514211a9.jpg"]]}
{"id":"be0be7ffc0544ece8e83d9db41c88938e9a4fb77694275d771b824272b8e2514","pubkey":"274a2e86f1a7acb45b01f290284a343c248cb913677b43d7f7615b513194d8b0","created_at":1781782986,"content":"https://share.yabu.me/274a2e86f1a7acb45b01f290284a343c248cb913677b43d7f7615b513194d8b0/31196ab8ae734f65a89fbee8507bb5c584dc4af50a00228d93216ff5dac9a384.webp","tags":[["imeta","url https://share.yabu.me/274a2e86f1a7acb45b01f290284a343c248cb913677b43d7f7615b513194d8b0/31196ab8ae734f65a89fbee8507bb5c584dc4af50a00228d93216ff5dac9a384.webp","m image/webp","x c7a1540808ff41656c75349f2059b6bd3505bbeb4c379c08208ed7e1a4426578","ox 31196ab8ae734f65a89fbee8507bb5c584dc4af50a00228d93216ff5dac9a384","size 95906","dim 1280x711","blurhash U8A0zL~o9F91VEskJRxsDjE4%2t15,IorZ$+","no_transform false","visibility 1"],["r","https://share.yabu.me/274a2e86f1a7acb45b01f290284a343c248cb913677b43d7f7615b513194d8b0/31196ab8ae734f65a89fbee8507bb5c584dc4af50a00228d93216ff5dac9a384.webp"],["client","lumilumi","31990:84b0c46ab699ac35eb2ca286470b85e081db2087cdef63932236c397417782f5:1727506446612","wss://cagliostr.compile-error.net"]]}
{"id":"0000077364434760c34f0ad28060c9d4cf1df010b563c821bf852258d8b989e4","pubkey":"3ffac3a6c859eaaa8cdddb2c7002a6e10b33efeb92d025b14ead6f8a2d656657","created_at":1781782923,"content":"https://blossom-espelhator.girino.org/3d4263b4a795e8b3279ee862bd0358bd5333fe701ec05fc9950cc1fb11717511.jpg\n\nThe Biggest Public Attack British Economists Ever Made on a Sitting Government\n\nOn 30 March 1981, 364 economists from across British universities signed an open letter to The Times declaring that the government's anti-inflation strategy had \"no basis in economic theory or supporting evidence.\" It was drafted by Frank Hahn and Robert Neild at Cambridge after Howe's March budget. The signatories included a future Governor of the Bank of England. \n\nWhat They Demanded\n\nThe letter said Thatcher's policy would deepen the recession and threaten Britain's social and political stability. It called for the rejection of monetarism in favor of \"alternative policies.\" In 1981, that meant the postwar Keynesian formula: more public borrowing, looser money, and state support for failing industry. Thatcher refused to reflate.\n\nBritain Was the Sick Man of Europe\n\nBy 1979 Britain had spent a decade falling behind every major European economy. Inflation hit 24 percent in 1975. The Callaghan government took a $3.9 billion IMF bailout in 1976, the largest in the Fund's history at that point. The Winter of Discontent of 1978-79 left rubbish uncollected and the dead unburied as public sector strikes paralyzed the country. The top marginal tax rate stood at 83 percent on earned income and 98 percent on investment income. The 364 wrote no letter against this. \n\nWhat Thatcher Actually Did\n\nShe rejected the Keynesian demand that Britain borrow and spend its way out of the recession. The top marginal income tax rate fell from 83 percent to 60 percent in 1979 and to 40 percent by 1988. The basic rate fell from 33 percent to 25 percent. Exchange controls were abolished in October 1979. British Telecom, British Gas, British Airways, and British Steel were sold to the public. The Employment Acts of 1980, 1982, and 1984 broke the unions' power to shut the country down. \n\nBritain Began Recovering in 1982\n\nGDP turned positive in 1982 and grew for the rest of the decade, peaking at 5 percent a year by 1988. British GDP per capita was growing faster than France's, Germany's, or Italy's, reversing decades of relative decline. The recession the 364 said would deepen ended within twelve months of their letter. \n\nThe Productivity Gap with Germany Closed for the First Time in a Generation\n\nIn 1979, West German output per hour worked stood 40 percent above Britain's. By 1989 that advantage had fallen to 17 percent. UK manufacturing output per worker rose roughly 78 percent over the broader period. Britain stopped losing ground to Europe and started catching up. \n\nThe Industries the Economists Said Couldn't Survive Were Replaced by Something Larger\n\nOn 27 October 1986 the Big Bang deregulated the London Stock Exchange. Fixed commissions ended and electronic trading began. Within a decade London had reclaimed its position as a top-tier global financial center. The capital the 364 said would vanish reorganized itself around higher-value activity. \n\nThey Never Retracted. They Were Promoted.\n\nNo collective retraction was ever issued. The signatories who later addressed the letter defended it as substantively correct, arguing the recovery happened despite the policy rather than because of it. Mervyn King, one of the 364, became Governor of the Bank of England. Stephen Nickell joined the Monetary Policy Committee. \n\nThe most prominent failed forecast in postwar British economics produced no professional consequences. \n\nThe Same Pattern Repeated in 2023\n\nIn October 2023, 108 economists including Thomas Piketty and Gabriel Zucman signed an open letter warning that a Milei victory would cause \"devastation\" in Argentina. \n\nHe won. Monthly inflation fell from 25.5 percent in December 2023 to roughly 2 percent by mid-2025. Poverty dropped from 52.9 percent in early 2024 to 28.2 percent in the second half of 2025. \n\nThe Mechanism Repeats Because Nobody Pays for Being Wrong\n\nA credentialed body issues a collective denunciation. Major outlets treat the letter as expert consensus. The policy succeeds anyway. No retraction follows, because the professional cost of being publicly wrong about a major economic prediction inside academic economics is approximately zero. \n\nThatcher Is Hated Because She Was Right\n\nThe results vindicated her and condemned her critics. People who build their identity around a vision of the world do not abandon that vision when the data contradicts it. They attack whoever the data vindicates. Her record is what their careers were built against. That is why the hatred has not faded in forty years.\n\nSource: https://web.facebook.com/photo/?fbid=1487198476772741&set=a.242077657951502","tags":[["nude_detector","https://blossom-espelhator.girino.org/3d4263b4a795e8b3279ee862bd0358bd5333fe701ec05fc9950cc1fb11717511.jpg sexual_activity 0.001"],["nonce","1400585","20"]]}
